---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Security — MeshWhisper"
  description="PQXDH, Double Ratchet, safety numbers, and the post-quantum roadmap."
>
  <Nav current="Security" />

  <!-- Page header -->
  <div class="border-b border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/40">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 text-center">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Security</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-400 max-w-xl mx-auto">
        Every app that integrates MeshWhisper gets post-quantum E2EE without having to understand
        or implement cryptography. These are the primitives underneath.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <main class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-20">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto space-y-20">

      <!-- Level table -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Security levels</h2>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800">
                <th class="text-left text-slate-500 font-medium py-3 px-5 w-16">Level</th>
                <th class="text-left text-slate-500 font-medium py-3 px-5">What's protected</th>
                <th class="text-left text-slate-500 font-medium py-3 px-5 w-28">Status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800">
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-white font-medium">1</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-slate-400">Classical E2EE — AES-256-GCM per message</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-brand-400 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide">Done</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-white font-medium">2</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-slate-400">Post-quantum at session establishment (PQXDH)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-brand-400 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide">Done</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-white font-medium">3</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-slate-400">Post-quantum healing within live sessions (PQ3 ratchet)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-5 text-yellow-500 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide">Roadmap</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs mt-3">
          Level classification matches Apple's iMessage PQ3 security levels. Signal is Level 2. Apple iMessage is Level 3.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- PQXDH -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">PQXDH — post-quantum initial key exchange</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Session establishment uses a hybrid of X3DH and ML-KEM-768. When Bob's pre-key bundle includes an
          ML-KEM-768 public key, Alice encapsulates to it during the handshake. The final shared secret mixes
          all X25519 DH outputs with the ML-KEM shared secret under a BLAKE3 domain separation context —
          matching Signal's PQXDH specification.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The protection: an adversary recording encrypted traffic today cannot decrypt it with a future
          quantum computer, because the session key was derived from an ML-KEM encapsulation that resists
          quantum attack. This addresses the "harvest now, decrypt later" threat model.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          The limitation: if ratchet state is compromised after establishment, future messages can be decrypted
          with classical compute. This is what Level 3 ratchet healing addresses.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Double Ratchet -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Double Ratchet</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Session encryption uses the Signal Double Ratchet. Every message uses a fresh key via a
          symmetric-key ratchet. When a new message arrives from the other side, a DH ratchet step
          advances the root chain using a fresh X25519 key exchange — providing forward secrecy and
          break-in recovery simultaneously.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          The implementation handles out-of-order delivery via a skipped-message-key store with a MAX_SKIP
          cap of 2,000 — sized to cover the relay's 72-hour store-and-forward TTL.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- PQ3 roadmap -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">PQ3 ratchet healing — Level 3</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Level 3 adds periodic ML-KEM injection into the running Double Ratchet. Every 50 messages —
          or every 24 hours — one side advertises a new ML-KEM-768 public key in the message header.
          The other side encapsulates to it and includes the ciphertext in the next reply. Both sides
          mix the resulting shared secret into the root key.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          The effect: even if ratchet state is compromised mid-conversation, post-quantum forward secrecy
          is restored at the next injection point. The exposure window is bounded by the injection cadence,
          not the session lifetime.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-yellow-800/40 rounded-xl p-5 text-sm text-slate-400">
          <span class="text-yellow-500 font-medium">Roadmap.</span> The spec is written —
          <a href="https://github.com/antonmannering/meshwhisper/blob/main/docs/pq3-ratchet-spec.md" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300 transition-colors">docs/pq3-ratchet-spec.md</a>.
          This is the next security milestone.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Safety numbers -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Safety numbers</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Both parties can verify a 60-digit safety number out-of-band to confirm no man-in-the-middle.
          Computed as a sorted BLAKE3 hash of both Ed25519 identity keys, formatted as 12 groups of 5
          decimal digits. Identical regardless of which party calls it first.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 font-mono text-sm text-slate-500">
          mw.getSafetyNumber(peerId)<br />
          <span class="text-slate-400">→ "12345 67890 12345 67890 12345 67890 ..."</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Threat model -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What the node knows</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A node operator can observe: source IPs of connected devices, push tokens for offline delivery,
          destination hashes (rotating hourly), and traffic timing. It cannot read message content,
          identify senders or recipients, or correlate traffic across namespaces.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          Private keys are generated on-device and never transmitted. The node never holds session keys
          or any key material from the Double Ratchet or PQXDH handshake. Media is encrypted locally
          before upload — the node stores ciphertext only.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Primitives -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Primitives</h2>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl divide-y divide-slate-800">
          {[
            ['@noble/curves', 'X25519, Ed25519 — elliptic curve operations'],
            ['@noble/ciphers', 'AES-256-GCM — authenticated encryption'],
            ['@noble/hashes', 'BLAKE3 — KDF, destination hashes, safety numbers'],
            ['@noble/post-quantum', 'ML-KEM-768 — NIST FIPS 203, PQXDH encapsulation'],
          ].map(([pkg, desc]) => (
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-baseline px-5 py-3.5">
              <code class="text-brand-300 text-sm w-44 shrink-0">{pkg}</code>
              <span class="text-slate-400 text-sm">{desc}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs mt-3">
          Audited pure JavaScript — no WASM, no native bindings. Identical in browser, Node.js, and React Native.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-slate-800 pt-8 flex justify-between text-sm">
        <a href="/how-it-works" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-slate-300 transition-colors">← How it works</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/antonmannering/meshwhisper/blob/main/docs/pq3-ratchet-spec.md" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300 transition-colors">PQ3 spec →</a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>

</Layout>
